@chapter 1 | Nature of the Economy and National Income
@unit UNIT I · ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT

@intro
This chapter opens the economy section with the **character of the Indian economy** (mixed, developing, the 1991 reforms) and then covers **national income**: who measures it (Dadabhai Naoroji, the Mahalanobis committee, the CSO and now the **NSO**), base years, real vs money income, per capita income as the measure of growth, state-wise income and **savings and capital formation**. Many old questions ask for "current" figures; the explanations give the latest data. It contains **{pyqs} PYQs and {ones} one-liners**.
@end

@section 1.1 | Nature of the Indian Economy

@q 0:1-3

A **mixed economy** has a **public sector and a private sector** working side by side, with the state guiding the market; India chose it in the 1950s (Industrial Policy 1948 and 1956). India is a **developing**, lower-middle-income economy. **Adam Smith** (*The Wealth of Nations*, 1776) is the father of economics; **J.M. Keynes** is the father of macroeconomics.

