@table
Milestone | Detail
**Dadabhai Naoroji** | First estimate of India's per capita income: **Rs. 20 in 1867-68** (*Poverty and Un-British Rule in India*)
V.K.R.V. Rao | First scientific estimate (1931-32)
**National Income Committee** (1949) | Chaired by **P.C. Mahalanobis** (with D.R. Gadgil, V.K.R.V. Rao)
**CSO** (1951) → **NSO** (2019) | Official estimates under the Ministry of Statistics (MoSPI)
Base years | 1948-49, 1960-61, 1970-71, 1980-81, 1993-94, 1999-2000, 2004-05, **2011-12** (from 2015, with GVA at basic prices); revision to **2022-23** under way
@end

@section 1.4 | Growth Rates and State Income

@q 1:18-22

These are "current affairs" questions of their year. Recent figures: India's real GDP grew **9.7% in 2021-22** (post-COVID rebound), **7.0% in 2022-23**, **9.2% in 2023-24** and about **6.5% in 2024-25**; India is the world's **fastest-growing major economy** and the **fourth largest** in nominal terms (about USD 4 trillion).

@q 1:23

@q 1:24

@q 1:25-27

@qnote 1:25 | The source keys (a) **Bihar**, which has had the **lowest per capita income** of the large states in most years: about **Rs. 69,000** (per capita NSDP, current prices, 2024-25) against a national average above Rs. 2 lakh. Its explanation mentions (c) for the year of the question, but Bihar is the accepted answer.

**Maharashtra** has the largest state economy, followed by Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, **Uttar Pradesh** and Gujarat. Among large states, **Telangana, Karnataka, Haryana and Gujarat** lead in per capita income; small states **Sikkim, Goa** and Delhi lead overall.

@up UP link: **Uttar Pradesh** has India's third or fourth largest GSDP (about **Rs. 25 lakh crore**, 2023-24) but a per capita income of only about **Rs. 97,000**, less than half the national average, because of its huge population. The state aims to become a **USD 1 trillion economy**; its schemes include **ODOP** (One District One Product, 2018) and the Global Investors Summits.

@section 1.5 | Savings and Capital Formation

@q 1:28-29

**Capital formation** depends on **savings** being channelled by **financial institutions** into **investment** (machines, buildings, infrastructure). Real per capita income grew slowly in the past because of **rapid population growth, inflation and slow farm and industrial growth**; foreign exchange shortage was not a main cause.

@q 1:30-34

India's **gross domestic savings** peaked at about **36-37% of GDP in 2007-08** and is now about **30%**. The **household sector** gives the largest share (about 60%), followed by private corporations and the public sector; households save more in **physical assets** (gold, land, houses) than in financial assets.

@trap
**Mixed economy = public + private sectors.** **Hindu rate of growth = 3.5%** (Raj Krishna). **Naoroji** made the first per capita income estimate (Rs. 20, 1867-68); **Mahalanobis** chaired the 1949 committee. National income is now estimated by the **NSO** (CSO + NSSO, 2019). Base year **2011-12** (revision to 2022-23). **Per capita income** measures growth and living standards. **Maharashtra** has the largest economy; **Bihar** the lowest per capita income. **Households** save the most.
@end

@heatmap

@pattern
National income questions mix **concepts** (real income, GDP vs per capita, who estimates) with **data of the year**, which date quickly. The 2021-2024 papers asked conceptual statement questions (capital formation, development vs growth, factors of growth), so learn definitions rather than old figures.
@end

